{"summary":"Current shrimp feeding and its unit test already operate on the routing organic-matter basis, so the two mass-basis amendments do not match the code as it exists. The real defect is the schema-3 to schema-4 migration leaving `reserve_g` at zero even though reserve now gates hatch and maturation behavior for loaded legacy saves.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-5-c3828380","decision":"reject","rationale":"`shrimp_feeding()` already converts demand and periphyton consumption onto the routing/organic-matter basis before detritus is touched: `food_demand_route_g`, `periph_consumed_route_g`, and `detritus_demand_route_g` are compared against and subtracted from `fine_detritus_g_total`. The claimed biomass-vs-organic-matter mismatch is not present in the current implementation."},{"amendment_id":"fr-5-d1fd0cd7","decision":"reject","rationale":"The cited test has already been updated to the routing basis. It computes `periphyton_consumed_route_g`, adds that to `detritus_consumed_route_g`, and compares the sum to `max_daily_intake_route_g` and `daily_food_consumed_g`. It is not summing incompatible periphyton-biomass and detritus-organic-matter values anymore."},{"amendment_id":"fr-5-847e2a07","decision":"accept","rationale":"`migrate_v3_to_v4()` is a no-op, so missing `animal.reserve_g` deserializes as `0.0`, and the existing save/load test explicitly locks that in. But reserve is now the gate for hatch and juvenile maturation via `fund_live_shrimp_biomass`, so a legacy save that previously had enough implicit retained biomass can load successfully yet have hatching or maturation blocked until feeding rebuilds reserve. That is a real backward-compatibility behavior regression, not just a harmless default."}]}